################################
AWS DMS Setup (End-to-End Guide)
################################

a. Create a Replication Instance (Free Tier)
Purpose: This acts as the "server" that performs the migration between Oracle and Aurora MySQL.

Steps:
Go to AWS DMS Console:
Open the AWS Console, search for Database Migration Service (DMS), and click it.
On the left menu, click Replication instances.
Create a New Replication Instance:
Click Create replication instance.

Fill in:
Name: dms-oracle-to-aurora (any unique name).
Description: Optional (e.g., "Free tier instance for Oracle to Aurora").
Instance class: dms.t3.micro (Free Tier eligible).
Storage: 50 GB (Free Tier allows up to 50 GB).
VPC: Choose the same VPC where your Aurora MySQL and Oracle DB (if in AWS) are located.

If Oracle is on-premises, pick a VPC with public access or a VPC connected via VPN/Direct Connect.
Publicly accessible: Yes (required if Oracle is on-premises).

Advanced Settings:
Multi-AZ: Uncheck (Free Tier only allows single-AZ).
Enable auto minor version upgrade: Check (optional).
Create the Instance:

Click Create.
Wait 5–10 minutes for the instance to become Available (status turns green).


3b. Configure Source Endpoint (Oracle)
Purpose: Connect AWS DMS to your Oracle database.

Prerequisites:

You’ve already granted SELECT ANY TABLE and LOGMINING to asutosh in Oracle (good!).

Supplemental Logging must be enabled in Oracle for CDC (Change Data Capture):

sql
-- Run this in Oracle as admin:
ALTER DATABASE ADD SUPPLEMENTAL LOG DATA;
ALTER SYSTEM SWITCH LOGFILE;
Steps:

Create Source Endpoint:

In the DMS Console, go to Endpoints > Create endpoint.

Endpoint type: Source.

Endpoint identifier: oracle-source.

Source engine: Oracle.

Server name: 10.124.51.233 (Oracle IP).

Port: 1521.

SSL mode: None.

Oracle connection details:

User name: asutosh.

Password: asutoshpassword.

Database name: XEPDB1 (your PDB name).

Advanced Settings:

Use DirectPath (Oracle): Checked (improves performance).

Add Column Name to Supplemental Logging: Yes (required for CDC).

Test the Connection:

Under Test endpoint connection, choose your replication instance (dms-oracle-to-aurora).

Click Run test.

Fix errors if any:

Common issues:

Security Group blocks port 1521: Ensure the replication instance’s security group allows outbound traffic to Oracle’s IP:Port.

Oracle listener not configured for remote connections: Check Oracle’s tnsnames.ora and listener.ora.

3c. Configure Target Endpoint (Aurora MySQL)
Purpose: Connect AWS DMS to your Aurora MySQL database.

Prerequisites:

Aurora MySQL cluster is already created (you mentioned this is done).

Aurora MySQL user permissions:

sql
-- Run this in Aurora MySQL as admin:
GRANT ALTER, CREATE, DROP, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, SELECT ON *.* TO 'asutosh'@'%';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
Steps:

Create Target Endpoint:

In the DMS Console, go to Endpoints > Create endpoint.

Endpoint type: Target.

Endpoint identifier: aurora-mysql-target.

Target engine: Amazon Aurora MySQL.

Server name: Aurora Writer Endpoint (e.g., cluster-abc123.cluster-xyz.us-east-1.rds.amazonaws.com).

Port: 3306.

SSL mode: None.

User name: Aurora MySQL admin username.

Password: Aurora MySQL admin password.

Test the Connection:

Choose your replication instance (dms-oracle-to-aurora) and click Run test.

Fix errors if any:

Common issues:

Aurora’s security group blocks inbound traffic: Allow port 3306 from the replication instance’s security group.

3d. Schema Conversion
Purpose: Convert Oracle schema (tables, data types) to MySQL-compatible schema.

Steps:

Download AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT):

Go to AWS SCT Download and install it.

Connect to Oracle (Source):

Open AWS SCT > File > New Project.

Source Database: Oracle.

Hostname: 10.124.51.233.

Port: 1521.

Username: asutosh.

Password: asutoshpassword.

Service Name: XEPDB1 (your PDB).

Click Test Connection > OK.

Connect to Aurora MySQL (Target):

Right-click Data Model Explorer > Add Target.

Target Database: Amazon Aurora MySQL.

Endpoint: Aurora writer endpoint.

Port: 3306.

Username: Aurora admin user.

Password: Aurora admin password.

Click Test Connection > OK.

Convert Schema:

Right-click your Oracle schema > Convert Schema.

Apply Data Type Mappings:

NUMBER → DECIMAL(38,0) (for integer-like columns).

VARCHAR2 → VARCHAR(4000).

Review Warnings: Fix any issues (e.g., unsupported functions).

Apply Schema to Aurora MySQL:

Go to File > Apply to Database > Choose Aurora MySQL.

Click Apply.



